  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Converting Between Reference Scales\n",
    "\n",
    "A potential has no absolute value. Every number you will ever read or report is a *difference* between the working electrode and some reference, and changing the reference changes the number without changing one thing about the chemistry.\n",
    "\n",
    "The arithmetic is trivial and people get it wrong constantly, because it is easy to add where you meant to subtract. The reliable way to think about it is as a ruler with several scales printed on it: one physical potential, one vertical line, several readings.\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large E_{(vs.\\ B)} = E_{(vs.\\ A)} + E_{A\\ (vs.\\ NHE)} - E_{B\\ (vs.\\ NHE)}\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "One scale in the figure below is not like the others. The **reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE)** is a hydrogen electrode sitting in the *same solution as your working electrode*, so its potential moves with the pH of that solution:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large E_{RHE\\ (vs.\\ NHE)} = -2.303\\frac{RT}{F}pH = -0.0592\\ pH \\quad (298\\ K)\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "That is why the water splitting literature quotes volts vs. RHE: on that scale hydrogen evolution sits at 0 V and oxygen evolution at 1.23 V at *every* pH, so the overpotential can be read straight off the axis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json": {
       "model_id": "354c87207696426790fc1c86b7b56ed8",
       "version_major": 2,
       "version_minor": 0
      },
      "text/plain": [
       "interactive(children=(FloatSlider(value=0.2, description='$E$ /V', max=1.8, min=-1.0, step=0.01), Dropdown(des…"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# echem.convert_reference(E, from_scale, to_scale) and echem.rhe_offset(pH)\n",
    "# do the arithmetic; echem.to_rhe(E, pH, from_scale) combines the two.\n",
    "# help(echem.convert_reference)\n",
    "\n",
    "TICKS = np.arange(-2.0, 2.01, 0.25)     # tick values marked on every rail\n",
    "X_MIN, X_MAX = -1.1, 2.0                # window, in volts vs NHE\n",
    "\n",
    "@interact(potential=widgets.FloatSlider(value=0.20, min=-1.0, max=1.8, step=0.01,\n",
    "                                        readout_format='.2f', description=r'$E$ /V'),\n",
    "          measured_vs=widgets.Dropdown(options=list(echem.REFERENCE_ELECTRODES),\n",
    "                                       value='NHE', description='measured vs.'),\n",
    "          ph=widgets.FloatSlider(value=0.0, min=0, max=14, step=0.5,\n",
    "                                 description='pH'))\n",
    "def plot_reference_ruler(potential, measured_vs, ph):\n",
    "    '''One physical potential, read off every reference scale at once.'''\n",
    "    e_nhe = echem.convert_reference(potential, measured_vs, 'NHE')\n",
    "\n",
    "    rails = list(echem.REFERENCE_ELECTRODES.items())\n",
    "    rails.append((f'RHE (pH {ph:.1f})', echem.rhe_offset(ph)))\n",
    "\n",
    "    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(7.0, 3.2))\n",
    "    ax = plt.subplot(111)\n",
    "    ax.set_xlim(X_MIN, X_MAX + 0.75)\n",
    "    ax.set_ylim(-len(rails) + 0.4, 0.9)\n",
    "    ax.set_yticks([])\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(r'$E$ /V vs. NHE')\n",
    "\n",
    "    for i, (name, offset) in enumerate(rails):\n",
    "        y = -i\n",
    "        ax.plot([X_MIN, X_MAX], [y, y], color=echem.color(i), linewidth=1)\n",
    "        for tick in TICKS:\n",
    "            x = tick + offset                       # where that reading sits on NHE\n",
    "            if not X_MIN <= x <= X_MAX:\n",
    "                continue\n",
    "            major = abs(tick % 0.5) < 1e-9      # label the half volt marks only\n",
    "            ax.plot([x, x], [y - 0.09, y + 0.09] if major else [y - 0.05, y + 0.05],\n",
    "                    color=echem.color(i), linewidth=0.8)\n",
    "            if major:\n",
    "                ax.annotate(f'{tick:.1f}', (x, y + 0.14), fontsize=5,\n",
    "                            ha='center', color=echem.color(i))\n",
    "        ax.annotate(name, (X_MAX + 0.05, y), fontsize=6, va='center',\n",
    "                    color=echem.color(i))\n",
    "\n",
    "    ax.axvline(e_nhe, color='red', linewidth=1)\n",
    "    ax.annotate('the same electrode', (e_nhe + 0.03, 0.6), fontsize=6, color='red')\n",
    "\n",
    "    print(f'A potential of {potential:+.3f} V vs. {measured_vs} is:')\n",
    "    for name, offset in rails:\n",
    "        print(f'   {e_nhe - offset:+.3f} V vs. {name}')"
   ]
  },
