  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Choosing a Reference Electrode\n",
    "\n",
    "The scale is only as trustworthy as the electrode that defines it. A useful reference electrode has three properties:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Non-polarizable.** Its potential must not shift when a little current passes. This is achieved by a fast, high-capacity couple with both forms present in excess: Ag/AgCl works because solid AgCl and solid Ag are both there, in a solution saturated with Cl$^-$, so drawing a small current changes nothing.\n",
    "- **A fixed internal composition.** From Lesson 9, the Ag/AgCl potential depends on $a_{Cl^-}$. That is why the filling solution is *saturated* KCl: saturation pins the activity even as solvent evaporates, provided undissolved KCl remains. A reference whose frit has dried out, or whose filling solution has been diluted by the test solution, silently reports a shifted potential.\n",
    "- **A small, stable junction potential.** The frit that separates the reference from your solution carries a potential of its own, typically a few mV, which adds to every reading. Saturated KCl is used partly because K$^+$ and Cl$^-$ have almost identical mobilities, which minimises that junction. Lesson 15 makes this quantitative.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two practical cases are worth knowing now:\n",
    "\n",
    "**Nonaqueous work.** Aqueous references leak water and chloride into organic solvents, and the junction potential across a water/organic boundary is large and irreproducible. The accepted fix is an internal standard: run your experiment, then add **ferrocene**, and report everything relative to the Fc/Fc$^+$ couple that appears in your own voltammogram. IUPAC recommends this precisely because it removes the junction from the problem entirely.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Pseudo-references.** A bare silver or platinum wire is often used in small cells and microelectrode work. It is stable over a short experiment but its absolute potential is unknown and drifts between sessions, so it is only defensible when paired with an internal standard.\n",
    "\n",
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-danger\">\n",
    "<b>Check your reference:</b> if a data set drifts steadily over hours, or a formal potential disagrees with the literature by tens of millivolts, suspect the reference electrode before you suspect the chemistry.\n",
    "</div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Batteries\n",
    "\n",
    "The battery technology challenge: match half-cell reactions to maximize voltage, energy density and charging rates while minimizing volume and mass.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-info\">\n",
    "<b>Activity:</b> Consider a Zn/MnO$_2$ (alkaline battery), which is based on the two half-cell reactions below. Which way do the reactions proceed on charging? Discharging?\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "$ZnO + H_2O + 2e^- \\rightleftharpoons Zn + 2OH^-$ ($E^0$ = -1.28 V)\n",
    "\n",
    "$2MnO_2 + H_2O + 2e^- \\rightleftharpoons Mn_2O_3 + 2OH^-$ ($E^0$ = 0.15 V)\n",
    "\n",
    "Hint: Remember that $\\Delta G_{cell} = -nFE_{cell}$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Pairing Half Cells: Voltage Is Not the Whole Story\n",
    "\n",
    "Combining two half cells to maximize $E_{cell}$ is the easy part. A battery also has to store as much energy as possible *per kilogram*, and that depends on how heavy the reactants are.\n",
    "\n",
    "One mole of electrons carries $F$ coulombs, so a cell at $E_{cell}$ volts delivers $FE_{cell}$ joules per mole of electrons. Divide by the mass of active material consumed **per mole of electrons** and convert to watt-hours per kilogram:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large \\text{specific energy} = \\frac{F E_{cell}}{3.6 (w_{anode} + w_{cathode})} \\quad [W h\\ kg^{-1}]\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $w$ is the **equivalent weight**, the grams of active material consumed per mole of electrons. Working per mole of electrons means any two half cells can be paired without rebalancing stoichiometry by hand.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a hard upper bound. It counts only the active materials, ignoring electrolyte, separator, current collectors and casing, so a manufactured cell delivers roughly a third of the number below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json": {
       "model_id": "b369d59901eb444b8b7dd249601a7dc2",
       "version_major": 2,
       "version_minor": 0
      },
      "text/plain": [
       "interactive(children=(Dropdown(description='cathode', index=8, options=('Li+/Li', 'Na+/Na', 'ZnO/Zn (alkaline)…"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# echem.BATTERY_COUPLES holds (E0 /V vs NHE, grams per mole of electrons) for\n",
    "# each half cell.  echem.theoretical_specific_energy does the arithmetic above.\n",
    "# help(echem.theoretical_specific_energy)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Three real cells, for comparison with whatever you build.\n",
    "BENCHMARKS = {\n",
    "    'alkaline (Zn/MnO2)': ('MnO2/Mn2O3 (alkaline)', 'ZnO/Zn (alkaline)'),\n",
    "    'lead-acid':          ('PbO2/PbSO4', 'PbSO4/Pb'),\n",
    "    'Li-ion (LCO/Li)':    ('LiCoO2', 'Li+/Li'),\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "def cell_summary(cathode, anode):\n",
    "    '''Cell potential and theoretical specific energy for one pairing.'''\n",
    "    e_cathode, w_cathode = echem.BATTERY_COUPLES[cathode]\n",
    "    e_anode, w_anode = echem.BATTERY_COUPLES[anode]\n",
    "    e_cell = echem.combine_half_cells(e_cathode, e_anode)\n",
    "    return e_cell, echem.theoretical_specific_energy(e_cell, w_anode, w_cathode)\n",
    "\n",
    "@interact(cathode=widgets.Dropdown(options=list(echem.BATTERY_COUPLES),\n",
    "                                   value='MnO2/Mn2O3 (alkaline)', description='cathode'),\n",
    "          anode=widgets.Dropdown(options=list(echem.BATTERY_COUPLES),\n",
    "                                 value='ZnO/Zn (alkaline)', description='anode'))\n",
    "def build_battery(cathode, anode):\n",
    "    '''Pair any two half cells and see what the pairing is worth.'''\n",
    "    e_cell, specific_energy = cell_summary(cathode, anode)\n",
    "    delta_g = -F*e_cell/1000                        # kJ per mole of electrons\n",
    "\n",
    "    print(f'Cathode: {cathode:28s} {echem.BATTERY_COUPLES[cathode][0]:+.2f} V vs. NHE')\n",
    "    print(f'Anode:   {anode:28s} {echem.BATTERY_COUPLES[anode][0]:+.2f} V vs. NHE')\n",
    "    print(f'E_cell = {e_cell:+.2f} V')\n",
    "    print(f'dG     = {delta_g:+.1f} kJ per mole of electrons')\n",
    "    print(f'Theoretical specific energy = {specific_energy:.0f} W h/kg of active material')\n",
    "    if e_cell <= 0:\n",
    "        print('\\nE_cell is not positive: this pairing is not spontaneous as written.')\n",
    "        print('Swap the two electrodes.')\n",
    "\n",
    "    fig = plt.figure()\n",
    "    ax = plt.subplot(111)\n",
    "    names = ['your cell'] + list(BENCHMARKS)\n",
    "    values = [specific_energy] + [cell_summary(c, a)[1] for c, a in BENCHMARKS.values()]\n",
    "    ax.bar(names, values, color=[echem.color(i) for i in range(len(names))])\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel(r'theoretical /W h kg$^{-1}$')\n",
    "    ax.tick_params(axis='x', labelrotation=20, labelsize=5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There are a very large number of different types of batteries that have been, and are being, explored. Some classic examples include:\n",
    "\n",
    "[Chem. Soc. Rev. 2009, 38, 2565](https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2009/cs/b820555h)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/battery1.png' width=50%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# Literature Examples\n",
    "\n",
    "### Studying reactions using $E_{OC}$\n",
    "\n",
    "[J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2013, 135, 3662.](https://doi.org/10.1021/ja3126432)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Experiment**: A voltage is applied to a Ni(OH)$_2$ coating on an electrode to oxidize it, then the circuit is broken. Monitoring $E_{OC}$ as a function of time reveals a spontaneous chemical reaction, enabling isolated analysis of its reaction kinetics. \n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/eoc.png' width=40%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Redox Flow Batteries\n",
    "\n",
    "The electrochemistry of small molecules has always been a subject of interest, but it has become even more popular in recent years. Redox flow batteries store the oxidized/reduced species in solutions, which provides scalability to these \"batteries\" that is absent in solid state batteries. The use of organic molecules enables researchers to capitalize on the synthetic tunability inherent in organic chemistry.\n",
    "\n",
    "[J. Power Sources 2017, 360, 243.](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpowsour.2017.05.057)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/redoxflow.png' width=50%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Inorganic Chemistry\n",
    "\n",
    "Along the line of synthetic tunability, the exchange of ligands in transition metal complexes is another effective way to manipulate electronic structure and reactivity. A beautiful paper from Barry Lever (York Univ.) in 1990 demonstrated highly predictable tunability of redox potentials through ligand manipulation. In essence, each ligand could be predicted to individually contribute a specific shift to $E^{0'}$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\large E^{0'} = 0.97 \\sum{E_i} +0.04 $\n",
    "\n",
    "[Inorg. Chem. 1990, 29, 1271](https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/ic00331a030)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/lever2.png' width=50%>\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/lever.png' width=50%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# Group Activity: Design a Battery, Then Defend It\n",
    "\n",
    "Everything in this lesson meets here. You have a voltage scale, a table of half cells, a definition of chemical reversibility and a tool that prices a pairing in watt-hours per kilogram. Now use them under a constraint, which is what the field actually does.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Form groups of three or four.** Each group takes one application:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Group | Application | What it demands |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 1 | Grid-scale storage for a wind farm | Thousands of cycles, cheap and abundant elements, mass and volume barely matter |\n",
    "| 2 | Electric vehicle | Maximum specific energy, must be rechargeable, safety under abuse |\n",
    "| 3 | Implanted medical sensor, ten year life | Tiny, absolutely no gas evolution, single use is acceptable |\n",
    "| 4 | Emergency backup that sits on a shelf for five years | Minimal self-discharge, must work immediately, moderate energy |\n",
    "\n",
    "For your application, work through all five steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Pick a pairing** from `echem.BATTERY_COUPLES` using the widget above. Report $E_{cell}$ and the theoretical specific energy.\n",
    "2. **Check the solvent.** Water is oxidized above 1.23 V and reduced below 0 V vs. RHE. Does your cell voltage fit inside that window? If not, you have committed to a nonaqueous electrolyte: say so, and accept the cost, the moisture sensitivity and the flammability that come with it.\n",
    "3. **Is it rechargeable?** Apply the test from the first half of this lesson: on reversing the current, do you recover the reactants you started with, or does something else happen first? A cell that is chemically irreversible is a primary battery, and Group 2 cannot use one.\n",
    "4. **Sanity check the mass.** Divide your theoretical number by three for a real device. Does it still meet the demand in the table?\n",
    "5. **Name one failure mode** your pairing has that the number does not capture: dendrites, gas evolution, dissolution of the active material, cost, toxicity, supply.\n",
    "\n",
    "Present in three minutes. The other groups' job is to attack the choice.\n",
    "\n",
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-info\">\n",
    "<b>Two questions for the whole class afterwards:</b>\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "1. The lead-acid benchmark in the widget is computed from the mass of PbO$_2$ and Pb alone, but sulfuric acid is a <i>reactant</i> in that cell and is consumed on discharge. Which way does including the acid move the specific energy, and why does that make the widget's lead-acid number more flattering than it should be?\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "2. Every group that maximised voltage probably reached for lithium. What does the potential scale figure say about why nobody built a practical lithium battery until nonaqueous electrolytes were developed?\n",
    "</div>"
   ]
  },
